<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William the Victorious</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1392–<a href="/years/1482">1482</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as William I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William the Victorious was Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> of the house of Welf. In the division of the Welf lands in 1432 he received the territory between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deister">Deister</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a>, which became the Principality of Calenberg, and he later also gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>. He earned his name in wars against neighbouring lords, and his sons later divided his lands again.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-the-victorious.jpg" alt="William the Victorious" width="226" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wilhelmdersiegreiche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William was the eldest son of Duke Henry the Mild of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> by his first marriage, to Sophia, a daughter of the Griffin duke Wartislaw IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. When his father died in 1416 he inherited the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, which he ruled jointly with his younger half-brother Henry the Peaceful. As a reigning prince he is counted either as William III or as William IV.</p><p>William proved an energetic ruler. He soon entered into feuds with neighbouring princes, among them the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a>, and he supported the Counts of Schauenburg and Holstein in their fight against King Eric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Fighting alongside Margrave Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, he shared in the defeat of the Hussite forces at the Battle of Brüx in 1421.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1428 William and Henry exchanged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> with their uncle, Duke Bernard I, for the Principality of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>. In 1432, while William was again away on campaign, his brother deposed him. After a bitter war between the brothers, Henry kept Wolfenbüttel and William was compensated with the territory west of the River Leine, which the Prince-Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildesheim">Hildesheim</a> cut off from the rest of the duchy. These lands took their name, the Principality of Calenberg, from his residence at Calenberg Castle.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> died out with Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-cocles">Otto the One-Eyed</a> in 1463, William took over their principality as well. After Henry the Peaceful died without sons in 1473, William again held both parts of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>. He then ceded the smaller principalities of Calenberg and Göttingen to his sons Frederick III and William the Younger and took up rule in Wolfenbüttel himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1423 William married Cecilia, a daughter of the Hohenzollern elector Frederick I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>; she died in 1449. In 1466 he married Matilda of Holstein-Schauenburg, the widow of Duke Bernard II of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. She died at Neustadt am Rübenberge on 22 July 1468, two days after giving birth to a son, Otto, who lived until 1471.</p><p>William died on 25 July 1482. His son Frederick the Turbulent succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> but was deposed in 1484, after which William the Younger ruled Wolfenbüttel, Calenberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> alone. The Principality of Calenberg that William had founded was raised, centuries later, to become the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William the Victorious, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1432 the territory between the Deister and the Leine, named after Calenberg castle, became a separate principality under <b>William the Victorious</b>, split off from the Principality of Wolfenbüttel.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-calenberg">Principality of Calenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Brunswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1450" aria-label="Brunswick on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of William the Victorious"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5548 5616h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5548 5616h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1450">What was here in 1450? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
